  <h2>What I owned</h2>
  <p>I was responsible for the backend architecture, implementation and production operations for several games. That work covered taking backend decisions from an initial design through implementation and into the day-to-day reality of operating software in production.</p>
  <p>As the sole backend engineer for these services, I worked across the technical choices that make a backend understandable, changeable and supportable while collaborating with the teams building the wider games.</p>

  <h2>Building Deathmatch from scratch</h2>
  <p>I built the Deathmatch backend from scratch. It used around 100 AWS Lambda functions, three APIs and event-driven workflows with SQS, EventBridge, OpenSearch and a single DynamoDB table.</p>
  <p>Those details describe the shape of the Deathmatch backend rather than the wider Illuvium platform. The work was about creating a service landscape that could support the game as it moved from an initial implementation into production operation.</p>

  <h2>Making the backend easier to operate</h2>
  <p>Alongside game features, I built idempotency, caching, analytics and operational tooling around the backend. These are the pieces that help a service handle repeated work safely, provide useful signals and remain practical to support after release.</p>
  <p>Production responsibility shaped the work from the outset: backend decisions needed to account for how the system would be run and changed, not only how an individual endpoint or feature would be implemented.</p>

  <h2>Revisiting earlier choices</h2>
  <p>As the backend evolved, I revisited earlier choices instead of treating the first implementation as fixed. I upgraded to .NET 9 and removed AutoMapper as part of work focused on Lambda cold starts.</p>
  <p>The point was to make a targeted runtime decision in response to the backend's operating context. It was practical maintenance work rather than a claim that a first design will stay right indefinitely.</p>

  <h2>Gauntlet: a separate backend story</h2>
  <p>I also owned the Gauntlet backend from the first line of code until the project moved to GameLift servers. That marks the boundary of the work described here; the Deathmatch architecture details do not apply to Gauntlet.</p>

  <aside class="article-callout">
    <p><strong>Scope matters.</strong> This case study focuses on my backend contribution. It does not publish internal architecture details, operational data or customer information.</p>
